eq \eta$ but $F_\eta(x_*) = F_{ho(s_*)}(x_*)$. By Proposition~ef{prop.V}, we have $\prod_{i=1}^n (\eta_i/ho_i(s_*))> 1$. Since $\eta$ and $ho(s_*)$ have $\eta_k$ in common, by the induction hypothesis, $F_\eta$ crosses $F_{ho(s_*)}$ exactly once, from below, at the same crossing point $x_*$ between $F_{ho(s_*)}$ and $F_	heta$. As in the proof of Theorem~ef{thm1}, we conclude that $x_*$ is the only crossing point between $F_\eta$ and $F_	heta$. The case of $s_*\in (-3, -2]$ is similar. Suppose $s_*\in (-2, 2)$. Regardless of which subinterval $s_*$ falls into, we have some $t^*\equiv(t_1^*, t_2^*)$ not identically zero such that  egin{equation} F_{	au(t^*)}(x) < F_	heta(x),\quad x\in (0, x_*);\quad F_{	au(t^*)}(x) > F_	heta(x), \quad x> x_*. \end{equation} By Proposition~ef{prop.V}, we must have $\prod_{i=1}^n 	au_i(t^*) \leq \prod_{i=1}^n \eta_i$, that is, $t^*\in \Omega$, with $\Omega\equiv \{t=(t_1, t_2):\ t_i \in [0, c_i],\ i=1,2;\ \prod_{i=1}^n \eta_i \geq \prod_{i=1}^n 	au_i(t)\}.$ If $t^*$ lies in the interior of $\Omega$, then repeating the entire argument with $	au(t^*)$ in place of $	heta$ (which corresponds to $t=(0, 0)$) we conclude that, either the claim does hold, or there exists $t_i^{**}\geq t_i^*$, with strict inequality for at least one $i=1,2$, such that $F_{	au(t^{**})}$ crosses $F_{	au(t^{*})}$ (and hence $F_	heta$) exactly once, from below, at $x_*$. And $t^{**}\in \Omega$. Let $\Omega_0$ be the set of $t\in \Omega$ such that (i) $t\geq t^*$, and (ii) $F_{	au(t)}(x)\leq F_{	au(t^*)}(x)$ for $x\in (0, x_*)$ and the inequality is reversed for $x> x_*$. By continuity, $\Omega_0$ is a closed set. Let $\omega^*$ be an element of $\Omega_0$ with maximal value of $\omega_1 + \omega_2$. The above discussion shows that, either the claim holds, or $\omega^*$ does not belong to the interior of $\Omega$, that is, $ \omega^*_i=c_i$ for at least one $i=1,2$. We can rule out the other boundary situation $\prod_{i=1}^n \eta_i =\prod_{i=1}^n 	au_i(\omega^*)$ in view of Proposition~ef{prop.V}, unless $	au(\omega^*)$ is a permutation of $\eta$, in which case the claim follows from the definition of $\Omega_0$ and the strict inequalities (ef{t.star}). In other cases, by the induction hypothesis, $F_\eta$ crosses $F_{	au(\omega^*)}$ exactly once from below, at $x_*$; the claim follows from this, the definition of $\Omega_0$, and (ef{t.star}).

